     content: 'The Y2K aesthetic is a specific nostalgia cocktail: frosted plastic, silver gradients, bubble letters, and the green phosphor glow of The Matrix. It is the visual language of 1999 through 2004 — the era of flip phones, AIM away messages, and "under construction" GIFs.\n\nUnicode captures several Y2K standbys. Monospace (𝚑𝚎𝚕𝚕𝚘) mimics the terminal text from The Matrix opening sequence. Circled letters (ⓗⓔⓛⓛⓞ) look like the frosted plastic buttons on a 2000s-era iMac remote. Small caps (ʜᴇʟʟᴏ) read like the condensed all-caps on a Motorola Razr screen. Fullwidth letters (ｈｅｌｌｏ) look like the system font from early Japanese mobile phones — a huge part of the 2000s tech landscape.\n\nThe Y2K revival on TikTok and Instagram has pushed these Unicode styles back into heavy rotation. The aesthetic works because it is familiar enough to trigger nostalgia but old enough that the details feel fresh again.',
