     content: 'Horror typography leans on the uncanny — letters that look wrong, broken, or from another century. The go-to Unicode set for horror is fraktur, the blackletter script (𝔥𝔢𝔩𝔩𝔬) that most readers associate with medieval manuscripts, occult texts, and gothic horror movies. Fraktur automatically signals "this is old, this is serious, this might be cursed."\n\nBeyond fraktur, you can layer unsettling effects by mixing character sets. A few inverted letters in an otherwise normal sentence create a subliminal wrongness. Combining double-struck outlines (𝕙𝕖𝕝𝕝𝕠) with regular text gives the impression of fading or ghostly lettering. Overlaying combining characters like combining macron or combining tilde can create a staggered, corrupted-text effect.\n\nThe horror genre in film and literature has a long history of distinctive title typography — from the Gothic serifs of classic Universal monster movies to the distressed, hand-scratched lettering of modern found-footage horror. These Unicode styles let you evoke that same atmosphere with nothing but a text input box.',
