     content: 'Fire text as an internet concept is less about literal flames and more about conveying intensity, heat, and "this goes hard" energy. Unicode covers this through a combination of heavy letter styles and directional symbols. The bold mathematical set (𝐚𝐛𝐜𝐝) reads as high-impact — like a shout in print. The fullwidth set (ａｂｃｄ) stretches letters wide like heat distortion. Combining these with the fire emoji (🔥) and upward-pointing symbols creates the visual equivalent of a hot streak.\n\nFor a literal flame aesthetic, the closest Unicode approach is layered: use bold or fullwidth text as the base, then accent with symbols that suggest heat — arrows pointing up (↑↗), spark symbols (✨), and the fire emoji (🔥). The combination reads as "fire" in the internet slang sense — excellent, intense, on a hot streak.\n\nThis style is everywhere on social media, particularly around achievements, hot takes, and things people are excited about. It is the Unicode equivalent of raising the energy level in the room.',
