     content: 'Fraktur is a style of blackletter type that was the most widely used typeface in German-speaking countries from the 16th century through the mid-20th century. Its distinctive broken strokes, compressed proportions, and decorative flourishes make it instantly recognizable as "old world" lettering — the kind you see on cathedral inscriptions, beer steins, and heavy metal album covers.\n\nUnicode includes a dedicated Fraktur block (𝔄𝔅ℭ𝔇𝔈𝔉𝔊...) as part of the Mathematical Alphanumeric Symbols. Despite the mathematical origin, these are genuine blackletter shapes that closely match the historic typeface. The set covers all Latin letters with a complete uppercase and lowercase alphabet.\n\nThis generator supports fraktur text, plus fraktur numbers using a best-available mapping. Numbers do not have dedicated fraktur codepoints in Unicode, so they fall through to regular numerals — a known limitation that affects all blackletter text generators.',
